---
// Projects — the build arc of the course: what your fork of
// cs-curriculum-main contains by June, plus the jam projects and the
// Create PT program. Adapted from the AP CS A Guide's projects page.
import Base from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<Base title="The Build — AP CS Principles (Unity)" active="projects" root="" docsHome="docs/index.html" css="style.css"
  description="Everything you build this year: the starter-project RPG, the boss fight, three game jams, and the AP Create performance task.">
<h1>The Build</h1>

<p>
    All year you build real, shippable software. First semester is one long
    project: your fork of <strong>cs-curriculum-main</strong> grows from a
    walking hero into a full game — a 2D RPG that becomes a 2D platformer
    when you enter a cave — ending with a boss fight that is entirely yours.
    Second semester you fork the <strong>Card-Game-Template</strong> for the
    card jam, and everything converges on the
    <a href="exam/create-performance-task.html">AP Create performance task</a>.
    Push to GitHub every single class.
</p>

<h2>Phase 1 — The Overworld (Sep-Nov)</h2>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>PlayerController.cs (v1)</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Lessons 1.3-1.6</span><span>Type: Starter Project</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        The script that runs the year: serialized speed fields, Input.GetAxis
        for direction, direction × speed × Time.deltaTime, transform.Translate,
        and the animation wiring (IsWalking, WalkDir, Attack).
        <a href="lessons/1.4.html">Build it →</a>
    </p>
</div>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>StatManager wiring (coins, health, potions)</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Lessons 2.1-2.4</span><span>Type: Starter Project</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        The starter's StatManager singleton tracks your run: coins tick up on
        trigger pickups, spikes knock health down on collision, potions heal
        with a max-health cap (your first <code>if</code>), and death halves
        your coins and resets you. Debug.Log is your print statement all
        semester.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>Turret.cs + TurretProjectile.cs</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Lessons 3.1-3.4</span><span>Type: Starter Project</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        Your first real adversary: a detection radius built from
        Vector3.Distance, rate-limited shooting with a cooldown timer, and
        projectiles that Instantiate, fly, and Destroy. Difficulty lives in
        serialized fields you tune in playtests.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>Enemy.cs (patrol, chase, attack, return)</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Lessons 4.1-4.5</span><span>Type: Starter Project</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        The full state machine: waypoint patrols, an aggro radius that flips
        it to chase, a close-range attack, and the reset back to patrol when
        you escape. Three distances, three behaviors — compound conditionals
        you can draw as a diagram.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>AxeItem, the door, and the cave</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Lessons 5.1-5.3</span><span>Type: Starter Project</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        Item pickups set inventory bools, the door checks them, UnityEvents
        open the way, and SceneManager.LoadScene swaps the RPG for a
        platformer — same PlayerController, different physics mode.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>Phase 2 — The Cave (Nov-Jan)</h2>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>PlayerController.cs (v2) — jump, double jump, ladders</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Lessons 6.1-7.4</span><span>Type: Starter Project</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        AddForce impulses, ground checks so you cannot fly, a jumps-remaining
        counter that the boots pickup raises to two, a climbing state for
        ladders, and feel polish: faster falls, coyote time.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>Your cave level (tilemaps)</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Lessons 7.1-7.7</span><span>Type: Level Design</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        A large multi-area cave built with the tile palette from the included
        CavesTileSet and CaveEntranceTileSet: collision, sorting layers, flow,
        difficulty ramp, secrets. Playtested by your classmates before break.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>BossController.cs — YOUR boss fight</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Lessons 8.1-8.7</span><span>Type: Your design</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        The first build that is fully yours: a written spec first (health,
        attacks, phases, win/lose), then the build — health bar, attack
        patterns, phase changes, and a player attack to fight back. The
        semester 1 final on Jan 21 is a normal B day: demo day plus a
        concepts check.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>Phase 3 — Data & Systems (Jan-Feb)</h2>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>Playtest analysis (data/playtests.csv)</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Lessons 9.4-9.6</span><span>Type: Data Analysis</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        Your own playtest data as a dataset: what happened in your cave,
        filtered, summarized, and argued — plus the bias and ethics roundtable
        that the AP exam loves to ask about.
        <a href="data/playtests.csv">Download the dataset →</a>
    </p>
</div>

<h2>Phase 4 — Jam Season (Feb-Apr)</h2>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>Jam 1: The 2-Button Game</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Feb 26 - Mar 15</span><span>Type: Game Jam</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        A complete game built on exactly two inputs. Pitched, built,
        playtested, polished, and shown in 3 weeks — the full development
        cycle the Create PT rubric calls CRD-2.E, compressed and real.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>Jam 2: The Card Game (your fork of Card-Game-Template)</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Mar 17 - Apr 9</span><span>Type: Game Jam + CSP Core</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        Lists, arrays, and ScriptableObjects: Card_data assets define your
        cards, GameManager holds decks and hands as Lists, and you fill in
        Shuffle(), Deal(), and AI_Turn(). A deck of cards is the best list
        on the AP exam — you will have built one.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>Phase 5 — The Create PT (Apr)</h2>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>Your Create PT program + video + PPR</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Apr 13 - May 3</span><span>Type: 30% of AP score</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        An individual program with a list, a parameterized procedure, and an
        algorithm with sequencing, selection, and iteration — built in 9
        in-class hours, captured in a video, and summarized in your
        Personalized Project Reference. Submitted to the AP Digital Portfolio
        by <strong>April 30, 11:59 PM ET</strong>.
        <a href="exam/create-performance-task.html">The full requirements →</a>
    </p>
</div>

<h2>Season 2 — After the exam (May-Jun)</h2>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>Jam 3: The Grid/Symmetry Game</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>May 17 - May 27</span><span>Type: Game Jam</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        Post-exam: build a game around a grid (match-3, 2048, minesweeper,
        tactics) or around symmetry (mirrored levels, twin-stick echoes).
        You know more than you think — prove it.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>Jam 4: Placeholder (topic TBA)</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Jun 1 - Jun 3</span><span>Type: Flex jam</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        A fourth jam runs here if the year allows — topic picked by the
        class. If snow days or slow jams eat the calendar, these days
        absorb the shift. The year ends with a retro: your September commit
        vs. your June build, side by side.
    </p>
</div>
</Base>
